## 10. Recovery Procedure

After confirming the simulated ransomware impact and Wazuh detection, the clean Restic snapshot was used to recover the affected test directory.

The recovery snapshot used during the validation was:

```text
1a006eb0
```

A dedicated recovery script was created:

```text
/usr/local/sbin/ag3-restore-demo
```

Recovery was initiated using:

```bash
sudo /usr/local/sbin/ag3-restore-demo 1a006eb0
```

The recovery process:

1. Connected to the protected Restic repository on BKP01.
2. Selected the clean recovery snapshot.
3. Restored the protected files into a temporary recovery location.
4. Replaced the affected ransomware demonstration folder with the clean restored copy.
5. Updated the Nextcloud file index.
6. Returned the test files to their original clean state.

Only the controlled ransomware test folder was restored. A complete Nextcloud server restoration was not required for this validation.

---

## 11. Nextcloud Recovery Verification

After restoration, the Nextcloud file index was updated using:

```bash
sudo -u www-data php /var/www/nextcloud/occ files:scan \
  --path="manager/files/Ransomeware-Demo"
```

The Nextcloud interface was then refreshed.

The original files became available again without the `.locked` extensions.

The simulated ransom note was also removed as part of the clean restoration.

This confirmed that the selected Nextcloud files could be successfully recovered from the protected Restic backup.

---

## 12. Wazuh Realtime Monitoring Issue Identified During Testing

During repeated ransomware and recovery testing, an issue was identified with Wazuh realtime monitoring after restoration.

The recovery process originally removed the affected directory using:

```bash
rm -rf "$TARGET"
```

and then recreated the directory using the restored backup.

Because Wazuh realtime FIM was monitoring the original directory, deleting and recreating the directory could cause the realtime filesystem watch to be lost.

After one restoration, new test files generated normal sudo logging events but initially did not generate the expected realtime FIM events.

The Wazuh agent on APP01 was restarted using:

```bash
sudo systemctl restart wazuh-agent
```

A second FIM test was then performed by creating:

```text
FIM-TEST-2.txt
```

Wazuh immediately generated:

```text
Rule ID: 554
Description: File added to the system.
Mode: realtime
```

This confirmed that realtime monitoring was working again.

### Operational Lesson

When a directory monitored by realtime FIM is completely deleted and recreated during a recovery operation, the Wazuh agent may need to refresh its filesystem monitoring.

For the final demonstration, the Wazuh agent was restarted after restoration before beginning another ransomware simulation.

This ensured that the restored Nextcloud directory was monitored correctly during the next test.

