## 15. Conclusion

The AG-3 ransomware recovery validation successfully demonstrated a controlled incident detection and recovery workflow.

The simulation showed that ransomware-like file changes within the Nextcloud environment could be detected by Wazuh File Integrity Monitoring and escalated through custom ransomware detection rules.

The affected files were then successfully recovered from a clean Restic snapshot stored using the MinIO backup infrastructure. After the recovery, the Nextcloud file index was updated and the original files became available again.

The testing also identified an important operational issue with Wazuh realtime FIM when a monitored directory is deleted and recreated during restoration. Restarting the Wazuh agent restored realtime monitoring, and this was incorporated into the final testing procedure.

Overall, the validation demonstrated the integration between Nextcloud, Wazuh, Restic, MinIO and the segmented AG-3 network in a practical cyber-resilience scenario.

> **Safety Note:** This validation used a controlled ransomware-impact simulation only. No real ransomware, malicious encryption or destructive malware was deployed.
